This list includes flags that either have been in use or are currently used by the [[United Kingdom]], the individual [[countries of the United Kingdom]], the [[British Overseas Territories]] and the [[Crown Dependencies]].
{{British flags}}

The [[College of Arms]] is the authority on the flying of flags in [[England]], [[Wales]] and [[Northern Ireland]] and maintains the only official register of flags for these countries.<ref name="CoA">{{cite web|url=http://www.college-of-arms.gov.uk/about-us|title=About Us|access-date=22 December 2012|publisher=College of Arms|quote=The College is also the authority for matters relating to the flying of flags, and holds the only official registers of flags for the UK and much of the Commonwealth.}}</ref> It was established in 1484 and as part of the [[Royal Household]] operates under the authority of [[the Crown]].<ref name="CoA" /> The [[Lord Lyon King of Arms]], established prior to 1399, holds a similar role within [[Scotland]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.lyon-court.com/lordlyon/238.html|title=Scottish Heraldic Flags|access-date=22 December 2012|publisher=The Court of the Lord Lyon}}</ref> A separate private body called the [[Flag Institute]], an educational charity financed by its own membership, also maintains a registry of United Kingdom flags that it styles 'the UK Flag Registry', though this has no official status under British law.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.flaginstitute.org/wp/flag-registry/|title=UK Flag Registry|publisher=Flag Institute|access-date=22 December 2012}}</ref>

==Flags recognised by planning law==
Certain classes of flag enjoy a special status within [[Town and country planning in the United Kingdom|English planning law]] and can be flown without needing the [[planning permission]] normally required for [[advertisements]]. These include any country’s [[national flag]], [[civil ensign]] or [[civil air ensign]]; the flag of the [[Commonwealth of Nations|Commonwealth]], the [[United Nations]] or any other international organisation of which the United Kingdom is a member; a flag of any [[List of islands of the United Kingdom|island]], [[Counties of the United Kingdom|county]], [[Districts of England|district]], [[Borough status in the United Kingdom|borough]], [[burgh]], [[parish]], [[City status in the United Kingdom|city]], [[town]] or [[village]] within the United Kingdom; the flag of the [[Black Country]], [[East Anglia]], [[Wessex]], any [[Parts of Lincolnshire|Part of Lincolnshire]], any [[Ridings of Yorkshire|Riding of Yorkshire]] or any [[Historic counties of England|historic county]] within the United Kingdom; the [[flag of St David]]; the [[flag of St Patrick]]; the flag of any administrative area within any country outside the United Kingdom; any flag of the [[British Armed Forces]]; and the [[Armed Forces Day]] flag.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |date=2021-07-20 |title=Flying flags: a plain English guide |url=https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/flying-flags-a-plain-english-guide/flying-flags-a-plain-english-guide |access-date=2025-08-15 |website=GOV.UK |language=en}}</ref>

Since the United Kingdom [[Brexit|left the European Union]], flying the [[Flag of Europe|flag of the European Union]] now requires planning permission.<ref name=":1" /><ref>{{Cite news |date=2023-06-23 |title=Brexit: City Hall unable to fly EU flag to mark vote anniversary |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-london-65991264 |access-date=2025-08-15 |work=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref> However the United Kingdom remains a member of the [[Council of Europe]], which uses the same flag.<ref>{{Cite news |date=2024-05-24 |title=Former MEP will continue flying European flag after feedback |url=https://www.bbc.com/news/uk-england-oxfordshire-69056186 |access-date=2025-08-15 |work=BBC News |language=en-GB}}</ref>

Certain other flags may be flown without obtaining planning permission, providing they adhere to certain restrictions including location, size, and number of flags. These include "house flags" for companies or individuals occupying a building, or temporary events taking place in a building; sports club flags (excluding sponsorship logos); the [[Rainbow flag (LGBTQ)|rainbow flag]]; flags of certain award schemes ([[Eco-Schools]], [[The King's Awards for Enterprise]], [[Investors in People]], [[Blue Flag beach|Blue Flag Award]] for beaches, and [[Green Flag Award]] for parks and open spaces); and NHS flags.<ref name=":1" />

